# LMFDB-sourced regression curves, in smalljac's INPUT format.
#
# This file is consumed by the (out-of-tree) smalljac driver, which counts
# points to produce good Euler factors and emits the rational.exe input format
#   label:degree:conductor:weight:[mus]:[[euler_factors]]
# documented at the top of examples/rational.c.
#
# Format of each data line (5 colon-separated fields; see the parsing loop in
# the removed examples/smalljac.cpp, git commit 660d3c8~1):
#   label:symdegree:conductor:curve_str:bad_euler_factors
# where
#   * label             LMFDB label of the curve.
#   * symdegree         symmetric-power degree. Always 1 here: we compute L(C)
#                       itself, not Sym^k L(C). (smalljac uses field 1 to drive
#                       the symmetric-power Euler-factor formulas for E/Q.)
#   * conductor         the arithmetic conductor of the L-function.
#   * curve_str         the defining equation:
#                         - elliptic curve  : [a1,a2,a3,a4,a6]  (5 a-invariants)
#                         - genus 2 curve   : [f(x), h(x)]  for y^2 + h(x) y = f(x)
#   * bad_euler_factors [[p1,p2,...],[Lp1,Lp2,...]] : the bad primes and their
#                       local L-factors Lp(T) = sum coeff_i T^i (low->high degree).
#
# Provenance, selection rationale, exact LMFDB queries, and the matching
# certified reference values are in README.md and reference.txt in this dir.
# Lines are sorted by (L-degree, conductor). Blank lines and #-comments ignored.
# L-degree = 2 for elliptic curves, 4 for genus 2 Jacobians (= 2*genus, with
# symdegree 1). It is derived by smalljac, not stored on the line.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# L-degree 2 : elliptic curves over Q, one per analytic rank 0..4
# curve_str = [a1,a2,a3,a4,a6];  bad factor 1-T (split), 1+T (non-split), 1 (additive)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.a2:1:11:[0,-1,1,-10,-20]:[[11],[[1,-1]]]
37.a1:1:37:[0,0,1,-1,0]:[[37],[[1,1]]]
389.a1:1:389:[0,1,1,-2,0]:[[389],[[1,-1]]]
5077.a1:1:5077:[0,0,1,-7,6]:[[5077],[[1,1]]]
234446.a1:1:234446:[1,-1,0,-79,289]:[[2,117223],[[1,1],[1,-1]]]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# L-degree 4 : genus 2 Jacobians over Q
# curve_str = [f(x), h(x)] for y^2 + h(x) y = f(x)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -- generic (geometrically simple, End = Q, Sato-Tate USp(4)), ranks 0..3 --
249.a.249.1:1:249:[x + x^2, 1 + x^3]:[[3,83],[[1,2,0,-3],[1,-1,83,-83]]]
587.a.587.1:1:587:[-x - x^2, 1 + x + x^3]:[[587],[[1,35,551,-587]]]
3319.a.3319.1:1:3319:[x^3 + 2*x^4 + x^5, 1 + x + x^3]:[[3319],[[1,-73,3245,3319]]]
25913.a.25913.1:1:25913:[-2*x - x^2 + x^3, 1 + x + x^3]:[[25913],[[1,-62,25974,-25913]]]

# -- square case: Jac ~ E x E, so L(C) = L(E)^2 (End_alg = M_2(Q)), rank 0 --
# every zero appears with multiplicity 2; a strong structural check.
196.a.21952.1:1:196:[1 + 3*x + 6*x^2 + 7*x^3 + 6*x^4 + 3*x^5 + x^6, x + x^2]:[[2,7],[[1,2,1],[1,-2,1]]]

# -- product case: Jac ~ E1 x E2 (non-isogenous), L(C) = L(E1) L(E2) (End_alg = Q x Q), rank 0 --
294.a.294.1:1:294:[x^2 + x^4, 1 + x^3]:[[2,3,7],[[1,2,3,2],[1,1,1,-3],[1,0,-1]]]
